# Target C — three-axis pentagon regression at the zero-detour atomic endpoint

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**  
**Status:** rigorous regression/counterexample to a proposed finite-pattern closure route; theorem authority unchanged.  
**Base:** `231fcc455f19c54974a3ffd1aaa27f00ca436a86`.

The audited-working §154 zero-outer-detour theorem gives the lower endpoint

\[
t_0:={\pi\over2}+{2\over5}\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\]

If one additionally assumes `t=(q-k)/D -> t_0`, the exact ledger forces

\[
{Q\over D}\to {4\over5}\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad
{Q+4f\over D}\to4\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad
Q-f=o(D).
\]

Thus almost every bounded defect face is a pentagon and the atomic endpoint-capacity
inequality is saturated. It is tempting to combine weighted-Mantel equality with the
`pi/3` phase relation and argue that a pentagon cannot terminate trains from the
resulting triangular-lattice directions without creating an exact unit triangle.

That implication is false. There is an explicit strict convex triangle-free unit
pentagon family converging to exactly that three-axis geometry, and its finite Oler
slack tends to zero as well.

---

## 1. Exact family

Take a parameter

\[
0<a<\pi/6
\]

and define `b=b(a)` by

\[
\boxed{\cos b=\cos a-{1\over2}.}
\tag{1.1}
\]

For sufficiently small positive `a`,

\[
{\pi\over3}<b<{2\pi\over3}.
\]

Let the five oriented unit edge vectors have arguments

\[
\boxed{
-a,\quad a,\quad \pi-b,\quad \pi,\quad \pi+b.
}
\tag{1.2}
\]

Their vector sum is zero:

\[
\begin{aligned}
&e^{-ia}+e^{ia}+e^{i(\pi-b)}+e^{i\pi}+e^{i(\pi+b)}\\
&=2\cos a-2\cos b-1=0
\end{aligned}
\]

by (1.1). Hence they form a closed equilateral pentagon.

The successive exterior turning angles, starting at direction `-a`, are

\[
2a,
\qquad
\pi-b-a,
\qquad
b,
\qquad
b,
\qquad
\pi-b-a.
\tag{1.3}
\]

For all sufficiently small `a>0`, every number in (1.3) lies strictly between `0`
and `2pi/3`. Therefore the closed polygon is strictly convex.

---

## 2. It is triangle-free

In an equilateral pentagon every diagonal joins vertices separated by exactly two
consecutive unit edges. If the exterior turn between those two oriented edges is
`delta`, the diagonal length is

\[
|u+v|=2\cos(\delta/2).
\tag{2.1}
\]

Every turn in (1.3) is strictly below `2pi/3`, so every diagonal satisfies

\[
\boxed{|u+v|>1.}
\tag{2.2}
\]

Thus the only unit distances among the five vertices are the five polygon sides. In
particular the pentagon contains no unit equilateral triangle.

This is exact for every sufficiently small positive `a`, not merely asymptotic.

---

## 3. Its directions converge to the three atomic phase axes

As `a downarrow0`, equation (1.1) gives

\[
b(a)\to\pi/3.
\]

The five projective edge directions in (1.2) therefore converge to

\[
0,\quad0,\quad {2\pi\over3},\quad0,\quad {\pi\over3}
\quad(\bmod\ \pi).
\tag{3.1}
\]

Hence every edge direction approaches one of the three triangular-lattice projective
axes

\[
0,\qquad {\pi\over3},\qquad {2\pi\over3}.
\]

At the singular limit `a=0`, one diagonal reaches unit length, but for every positive
`a` all diagonals remain strictly longer than one. Therefore asymptotic three-axis
phase concentration does **not** force an exact triangle at finite `D`.

---

## 4. The per-face Oler slack also vanishes

Let `A(a)` be the pentagon area and put

\[
a_0={\sqrt3\over2},
\qquad c_0={\sqrt3\over4}={a_0\over2}.
\]

For a unit pentagon with no interior source points, the finite Oler slack is

\[
\omega_5(a)
:=A(a)+5c_0-4a_0
=A(a)-{3\over2}a_0.
\tag{4.1}
\]

As `a downarrow0`, the polygon converges to the degenerate five-edge boundary with
edge arguments

\[
0,0,{2\pi\over3},\pi,{4\pi\over3}.
\]

Its vertices may be taken as

\[
(0,0),\ (1,0),\ (2,0),
\left({3\over2},{\sqrt3\over2}\right),
\left({1\over2},{\sqrt3\over2}\right),
\]

whose area is

\[
{3\sqrt3\over4}={3\over2}a_0.
\]

Area is continuous under vertex convergence, so

\[
\boxed{\omega_5(a)\to0.}
\tag{4.2}
\]

Thus a linear family of near-three-axis pentagons need not pay any fixed positive Oler
slack per face.

---

## 5. Consequence for the live endpoint attack

The following proposed implications are invalid without additional global structure:

1. `three atomic train directions + pentagonal stopping faces -> exact unit triangle`;
2. `three atomic train directions + pentagonal stopping faces -> fixed positive phase gap`;
3. `triangle-free pentagon -> fixed positive Oler slack`.

The family above simultaneously regresses all three.

A viable continuation at the §154 endpoint must therefore use information not present
in one face in isolation, for example

- compatibility of many pentagons with the global stopped-train order;
- the exceptional-face closure-rank/Hall theorem;
- KKT diameter bracing of the outer-to-outer train population; or
- a collective deformation of the near-lattice pentagon network.

**Disposition:** rigorous route-killing regression. C-II remains open.